      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix wholemeal flour with 1 teaspoon of salt and enough water to make a soft dough. Knead 5 minutes. Cover and rest 30 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix mashed potato with chilli, ginger, cumin, coriander, garam masala, fresh coriander, lemon juice, and remaining salt. Divide into 8 balls."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide dough into 8 balls. Roll one ball into a 10 cm circle. Place a filling ball in the centre. Bring the edges up and seal tightly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Gently roll the stuffed ball into a 18 cm circle. Roll slowly and evenly to avoid the filling breaking through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat a tawa over medium-high heat. Cook the paratha 2 minutes. Flip, add a teaspoon of ghee, and press with a spatula. Cook 2 minutes. Flip once more, add more ghee, and cook until golden on both sides."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Finish with a small piece of butter on top. Serve with plain yoghurt and mango chutney."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Filling bursts through the dough while rolling",
              "cause": "Dough rolled too thin too quickly, or filling was wet",
              "fix": "Patch with a small piece of dough. Next time, ensure the mashed potato is very dry before mixing the filling."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Paratha is chewy and raw-tasting",
              "cause": "Not enough cooking time or heat was too low",
              "fix": "Cook on a properly hot tawa. The paratha should take a maximum of 2 minutes per side. Increase heat."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Aloo paratha is eaten as a morning meal from Punjab to Uttar Pradesh. In Amritsar, the Punjab city most associated with the dish, dedicated paratha shops serve thick, butter-drenched versions. The Paranthe Wali Gali in Delhi's Chandni Chowk is a narrow alley of paratha shops that have been serving stuffed parathas since the nineteenth century. The paratha is always served with a white butter called makhan, yoghurt, and sometimes a sweet pickle."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
